{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Also</b> — like: גם בשחוק יכאב לב, whose sense is like עד." "<b>And the swallow</b> — the name of a singing bird; perhaps it is so called because its habit is not to sing so long as it is not free [דרור]. This bird is known in Spain. Rabbi Moses said: since the bird's nature is to flee from human settlement, it circles toward the place of glory, the place of the offerings brought before Him — by way of parable, and they do so — 'perhaps they drew near to Your altar,' for they know the place of the altar; therefore it lays its fledglings there. And Rabbi Judah ben Balaam mocked Rabbi Moses: for what intellect do birds have?! Rather the interpretation is: 'My soul longs for the courts of YHWH and for Your altars,' yet I wander and do not find what I seek — and behold, the bird has found a house and a nest, while I do not find."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 84, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:36:01.433732479Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}